const c=(id,title,lede,takeaway,depth,source,question,options,answer,explanation)=>({id,title,lede,lab:id,takeaway,depth,source,question,options,answer,explanation});
export const databaseLessons=[
 {slug:'storage',title:'Rows become pages',tag:'01 / Physical storage',summary:'Slotted pages, buffer pools, row and column layouts, and compression you can inspect.',chapters:[
 c('pages','A row lives inside a page.','Insert rows, delete one, and compact the page. Watch payloads move while slot numbers stay put.','Logical rows become bytes, slots, and pages. Free space can exist without being contiguous enough for a new record.',[
 ['Follow the two fronts','The toy reserves a header, grows slot pointers from the left, and packs row payloads from the right. A slot gives indirection: compaction can move a payload and update its pointer. Here deletion merely marks a row dead; compaction reclaims its bytes.'],
 ['Real page formats','Real records also carry metadata, alignment, null maps, and version information. Large values may be compressed or stored out of line, making a narrow-looking query fetch more pages. PostgreSQL’s physical tuple location is not a permanent application identifier; use logical keys.']
 ],['pages','toast'],'Why keep slot pointers when row payloads move?',['To locate a row without fixing its payload at one byte offset','To eliminate all row metadata','To make every row the same size'],0,'Indirection lets the slot stay stable while its offset is updated during compaction.'),
 c('buffers','A database reads through a buffer pool.','Read and dirty pages, then exceed the pool. An eviction may need a write before the next read.','A cache miss and a dirty eviction have different costs. Read locality, writeback, and memory pressure interact.',[
 ['Observe the state','Each frame holds one page. A hit avoids a modeled storage read; a miss brings the page into the pool. A modified page is dirty until written back. The toy evicts the least recently accessed page and safely writes dirty victims first.'],
 ['Several layers of caching','A database buffer miss can still hit the OS page cache. Real engines use replacement policies, pinning, latches, prefetch, and background writers that this toy omits. PostgreSQL does not use this exact LRU algorithm. Count physical I/O, buffer activity, and waits separately.']
 ],['buffers'],'A dirty page is selected for eviction. What extra work may be required?',['Writing the modified page safely before reusing its frame','Dropping the transaction history','Reading every table'],0,'The frame contains changes not yet reflected in the data file; overwriting it without preservation would lose them.'),
 c('layouts','Store together what you read together.','Scan a wide table while changing how many columns the query needs.','Row and column layouts favor different access patterns. Minimize the bytes the workload must touch, not just the number of rows returned.',[
 ['Projection changes traffic','A row layout places a record’s fields together. A column layout groups values of the same field, allowing a scan to omit unneeded columns. This lab scans every row, with eight-byte fields and no compression. It isolates projection; an indexed point lookup is a different workload.'],
 ['Execution follows layout','Columnar engines can process vectors of values and push filters into scans. Row-oriented engines often suit small transactional reads and writes. Neither label determines every trade-off: indexes, update handling, row groups, compression, and physical clustering also matter.']
 ],['layout'],'A full scan needs one of eight fixed-width columns. Which layout can omit the other seven here?',['Column layout','Both must always read every field','Neither can project columns'],0,'Separate column storage allows this scan to fetch only the requested column’s pages.'),
 c('encoding','Repeated values can become smaller instructions.','Compare raw values, dictionary IDs, and runs. Rearrange the same values without changing their frequency.','Compression depends on both cardinality and order. Saving I/O can justify decoding work, but a poor encoding may expand the data.',[
 ['Decode the representation','A dictionary stores each distinct eight-byte value once, then one-byte IDs. Run-length encoding adds a one-byte run length beside each ID. Clustered values create long runs; interleaved values may need a run per row. Sizes exclude headers and are deliberately simple.'],
 ['Choose by the data','Real systems select encodings by segment: bit packing, deltas, dictionaries, runs, or a general codec. Some operators can work directly on encoded values. Compare compression, decode CPU, random-access cost, and update behavior rather than maximizing a ratio alone.']
 ],['encoding'],'Two columns have the same values and frequencies. Can run-length sizes still differ?',['No, frequency completely determines size','Yes, neighboring equal values form longer runs','Only if the data is corrupted'],1,'Run lengths depend on order. A dictionary’s value set alone does not capture that order.')
 ]},
 {slug:'indexes',title:'Find data without scanning it all',tag:'02 / Access paths',summary:'Grow a B+ tree, scan composite keys, follow LSM versions, and test Bloom-filter uncertainty.',chapters:[
 c('btree','A split grows the search structure.','Insert keys into tiny leaf pages. Follow the separators when a page overflows.','A B+ tree keeps leaf keys ordered and search paths balanced. Page splits preserve the search invariant while creating write work.',[
 ['Watch the boundary','All records live in leaves here. Internal separators equal the smallest key in the subtree to their right. Equality routes right. An overflowing leaf splits; an overflowing parent can split too, increasing height only when the root splits. Range scans continue through ordered leaves.'],
 ['Why high fanout matters','Real pages hold many entries, keeping trees shallow. Wide keys, included payload, fill factor, and insertion patterns affect occupancy and write cost. Unique constraints, concurrent splits, recovery, and deletion require machinery beyond this single-threaded insertion toy.']
 ],['btree'],'A key equals an internal separator. Which subtree contains it in this toy?',['The left subtree','The right subtree','Neither subtree'],1,'Separators are copied from the right subtree’s minimum key; equality routes right.'),
 c('composite','Key order shapes the interval you can seek.','Find one tenant’s recent rows. Reverse the index columns and compare the entries visited.','Equality on a leading key followed by a range often defines a tight interval. A covering index can also reduce heap access, at a write and space cost.',[
 ['Lexicographic order','The lab sorts by (tenant, day) or (day, tenant). With tenant equality first, the day range stays inside one tenant. With day first, this basic range scan visits other tenants and filters them. Engines may have skip scans or other strategies; this lab does not model them.'],
 ['Covering does not always mean heap-free','Included payload can satisfy a projection without storing it in the search key. PostgreSQL index-only scans may still consult heap pages when visibility is not established by the visibility map. Partial and expression indexes can target a workload, but predicates and expressions must match usable query conditions.']
 ],['composite','covering'],'An index contains every projected field. Is a PostgreSQL index-only scan guaranteed to avoid every heap visit?',['Yes','No, visibility checks may still need heap pages','Only if the index is a hash index'],1,'Covering the values and proving tuple visibility are separate requirements.'),
 c('lsm','New values accumulate above old ones.','Write, flush, overwrite, and delete a key. Search the newest source first.','An LSM tree moves write work into immutable sorted runs and later compaction. Reads must reconcile versions and tombstones.',[
 ['Follow a write','The toy stores the newest in-memory value for each key. A flush creates an immutable sorted run. A newer tombstone means deleted even if an older run contains a value. Real engines commonly combine this with a WAL; flushing the memtable and syncing the WAL are different events.'],
 ['Work is deferred','Accumulating runs makes writes easy now but increases lookup and merge work later. Compaction trades write amplification for read and space efficiency. A storage engine is one component: RocksDB’s key/value engine is not a complete relational planner and SQL transaction system.']
 ],['lsm'],'The newest entry for a key is a tombstone; an older run contains a value. What should a latest-value read return?',['The older value','Not found','Both values as current'],1,'The deletion marker overrides the older version. Continuing past it would resurrect deleted data.'),
 c('bloom','Sometimes “maybe” saves a disk read.','Hash keys into a bit array. Probe present and absent keys, then search for a collision.','A standard Bloom filter can prove absence, but a positive result still needs a real lookup. It stores membership hints, not values.',[
 ['Read the bits','Inserting a key sets every position selected by its hashes. Querying checks the same positions. Any zero proves absence; all ones mean maybe present. Different keys can set overlapping bits. More bits often reduce false positives for a fixed workload, while more hashes also cost CPU.'],
 ['What the guarantee assumes','The filter must include the keys being queried and be read correctly. Plain filters cannot safely delete a key by clearing its bits because other keys may share them. In an LSM engine, per-run filters can avoid reading runs that certainly lack a key.']
 ],['bloom'],'All tested bits are set. What have you learned?',['The key definitely exists','The key might exist; check the underlying data','The stored value is all ones'],1,'Other inserted keys may have set those bits. A positive filter result is not proof of presence.')
 ]},
 {slug:'queries',title:'Turn SQL into physical work',tag:'03 / Query execution',summary:'Cardinality estimates, access costs, join strategies, memory spills, and stable pagination.',chapters:[
 c('plans','A plan is a bet on how many rows survive.','Give the optimizer a stale estimate, then reveal the actual selectivity.','Cardinality errors propagate into access paths, joins, and memory use. Compare estimated and actual rows before tuning cost knobs.',[
 ['Follow the cost comparison','The toy chooses between a fixed-cost sequential scan and an index scan whose random page visits grow with selectivity. Estimated rows choose the plan; actual rows determine its realized cost. A broad index scan can cost more than reading the table sequentially.'],
 ['Statistics are a model','Histograms, common values, null fractions, and distinct counts describe distributions. Independent-column assumptions fail for correlated predicates; extended statistics may help. EXPLAIN ANALYZE executes the statement: use care with writes. Inspect rows, loops, buffers, and waits, not only the plan’s top line.']
 ],['stats','plans'],'An index scan was chosen for 1% estimated selectivity but actually touches half the table. What should you inspect first?',['Statistics and the row estimate','Whether every table has more indexes','Only the SQL text length'],0,'A wrong cardinality estimate can make an expensive access path look cheap.'),
 c('joins','A join chooses how to find matches.','Change the relation sizes, an inner index, and input ordering. Compare work before choosing an algorithm.','Join strategy depends on cardinality, ordering, memory, and available access paths. Filter placement and join order can change all four.',[
 ['Three physical strategies','Nested loops search the inner input per outer row; an index can make each search cheap. A hash join builds a key table on one input and probes it with the other. Merge join walks ordered inputs; producing that order can dominate its cost.'],
 ['Read the surrounding plan','The diagram assumes one match per outer key and abstracts away output cost. Many-to-many matches, skew, spills, and parallelism change the calculation. SQL predicates can often move down to reduce inputs, but outer joins, null semantics, and volatile expressions constrain legal rewrites.']
 ],['plans'],'A nested-loop join has a tiny outer input and an indexed inner input. Must it be worse than a hash join?',['Yes, nested loops are always quadratic','No, a few index probes can be cheaper than scanning the inner relation','Yes, indexes cannot serve joins'],1,'The access path changes the inner operation from a full scan to a targeted lookup.'),
 c('spills','An operator can outgrow its memory.','Sort a relation with a fixed merge fan-in. Shrink the memory budget and watch extra passes appear.','Memory pressure changes the algorithm, not just its speed. Per-operation budgets multiply across operators, workers, and concurrent queries.',[
 ['Follow external sorting','Create sorted runs that fit in memory, write them, then merge groups of runs. Intermediate merges can write another generation. The final merge streams results instead of materializing another temporary file in this toy. A fit-in-memory sort needs no temporary I/O.'],
 ['Other blocking operators','Hash joins and hash aggregation also need state and may partition or spill. ORDER BY, DISTINCT, and grouping can block output while building that state. Vectorized execution reduces per-row overhead but does not make state free. Inspect spill bytes and operator concurrency before globally raising memory limits.']
 ],['memory'],'A query has several memory-hungry operators and many copies run concurrently. What is wrong with treating one operator’s budget as total server memory use?',['Nothing; one budget covers the server','Budgets can be consumed by multiple operators and workers at once','Sorting never allocates memory'],1,'The relevant bound includes concurrent operator instances and their actual allocation behavior.'),
 c('pagination','A cursor follows a key, not a position.','Read five rows, insert newer rows, then request the next page.','A deterministic unique order is essential. Keyset pagination avoids deep offset skipping and can resist position shifts, but it does not create a fixed snapshot.',[
 ['Observe the duplicate','This descending feed uses a unique immutable ID. OFFSET 5 counts from the current beginning; new rows shift that boundary. A cursor asks for IDs below the last returned ID, so the next range remains anchored to a value.'],
 ['Define the contract','For a nonunique timestamp, use a unique tiebreaker such as (created_at, id), a matching index, and a cursor containing both. Mutable sort keys can still move rows across the cursor. A consistent historical traversal needs snapshot semantics or another stable boundary. Keyset access also gives up cheap arbitrary page-number jumps.']
 ],['pagination','composite'],'Two newer rows arrive between pages. Why can OFFSET repeat rows?',['The positions of previously returned rows shifted','Indexes always return duplicates','The database forgot the primary key'],0,'OFFSET counts current positions. It does not remember which row ended the prior page.')
 ]},
 {slug:'transactions',title:'Give concurrent work a meaning',tag:'04 / Transactions',summary:'MVCC visibility, write skew, lock waits, and constraints that close application-level races.',chapters:[
 c('snapshots','A snapshot chooses which version exists for you.','Keep reading while another transaction commits a new value. Change the snapshot boundary.','MVCC allows readers to see a consistent version without treating every newer committed value as immediately visible.',[
 ['One logical row, several versions','The timeline uses commit sequence numbers: a snapshot reads the newest version committed by its cutoff. This is a teaching abstraction, not PostgreSQL’s actual transaction-ID visibility algorithm. Uncommitted versions are excluded.'],
 ['Statement versus transaction scope','Here read committed refreshes the cutoff for each read; repeatable read keeps the transaction’s first cutoff. Real engines differ in isolation guarantees. Long snapshots retain older versions, so read-only work can still impose cleanup and storage costs.']
 ],['mvcc','isolation'],'A repeatable-read transaction keeps its original snapshot. Another transaction commits an update. The next ordinary snapshot read sees…',['The version visible in the original snapshot','Necessarily the newest committed version','An uncommitted intermediate value'],0,'A later commit does not move this transaction’s retained snapshot boundary.'),
 c('write-skew','Two valid snapshots can break one invariant.','Two reservations each consume a different final resource after checking the other. Step through both commits.','A consistent snapshot is not automatically serializable. Protect the cross-row invariant with a suitable transaction or constraint strategy.',[
 ['Disjoint writes still interact','A and B each read that the other resource remains available, then mark their own unavailable. Their write sets do not overlap, but together they leave none available. The toy’s serializable mode rejects one transaction to prevent that outcome.'],
 ['Choose the boundary deliberately','Serializable transactions may require retrying the whole transaction after an abort. A shared guard row or a correct predicate-locking strategy can also serialize the critical decision. Locking only rows that currently exist may miss a predicate or an empty-range race.']
 ],['isolation','locks'],'Both transactions update different rows. Does that rule out an isolation anomaly?',['Yes','No, their decisions can depend on each other’s rows','Only if each transaction reads no data'],1,'The invariant spans rows, so disjoint write sets do not imply independent decisions.'),
 c('locks','Waiting is a state you must inspect.','Let one transaction keep a row lock open while another tries to update the same row.','A slow query may be waiting for another transaction rather than doing expensive computation. Find the blocker and its ownership.',[
 ['A lock outlives the statement','In this toy, A’s update statement finishes but its transaction retains the row lock. B waits until A commits or rolls back. The waiting time is part of B’s latency even if B’s own update is tiny.'],
 ['Make progress predictable','Acquire locks in a consistent order, keep transactions short, and avoid external calls while holding them. Deadlock detection can abort a participant; lock timeouts and cancellation also require error handling. Ordinary readers and writers interact differently under different engines and lock modes.']
 ],['locks'],'An UPDATE completed, but its transaction is still open. Can it keep another UPDATE waiting?',['Yes, its row lock can last until transaction end','No, all locks disappear after each statement','Only when the table has no index'],0,'Statement completion and transaction completion are separate milestones.'),
 c('constraints','Checking first does not reserve the result.','Two clients both see a free seat. Let both insert, then add a uniqueness boundary.','Express invariants in the database when possible. Application checks can improve feedback but cannot replace atomic enforcement under concurrency.',[
 ['Make the race visible','Both clients inspect the same initially empty seat. Without enforcement, both insert a reservation. With a unique key on the seat, only one succeeds in this simplified sequence. Real concurrent enforcement can wait before reporting a conflict.'],
 ['Model the invariant precisely','Primary keys identify rows; foreign keys preserve references; NOT NULL and CHECK enforce local requirements. A CHECK accepting unknown is not a substitute for NOT NULL. NULL uniqueness rules are engine-specific. Normalize shared facts to reduce inconsistent updates, and justify any duplicated or derived data with a maintenance strategy.']
 ],['constraints'],'Two clients both checked that a seat was free. What prevents both committed reservations?',['Relying on both checks being fast','A unique constraint on the correct seat identity','Adding a SELECT before every INSERT'],1,'The database must enforce the invariant at the write boundary, where concurrent attempts meet.')
 ]},
 {slug:'recovery',title:'Survive the interrupted write',tag:'05 / Durability',summary:'Write-ahead ordering, checkpoint costs, idempotent redo, and recovery to a point before a mistake.',chapters:[
 c('wal','The log gets durable before the data page.','Try to flush the data too early. Then crash before and after a durable commit record.','A durable log can preserve a committed change before its data page reaches disk. Acknowledgment policy determines the client’s guarantee.',[
 ['Two ordering rules','Before a dirty page is written, its required log must be durable. For a synchronous durable commit, acknowledgment waits for the commit record to become durable. Several transactions may share a log sync; every changed page need not be flushed at each commit.'],
 ['An uncertain reply','A crash after durable commit but before acknowledgment can leave the client unsure even though recovery preserves the change. Use stable operation identity when retrying uncertain writes. This lab assumes storage honors sync and does not model replication, torn pages, or asynchronous commit.']
 ],['wal'],'The commit log is durable but the new data page is not. Can recovery retain the committed change?',['Yes, by replaying the durable log','No, every data page must be synced at commit','Only if the client saw a response'],0,'Write-ahead logging lets recovery reconstruct a committed page change from durable records.'),
 c('checkpoints','A checkpoint bounds how far recovery looks back.','Keep updating six pages. Change checkpoint frequency and compare page writes with the redo distance.','More frequent checkpoints can shorten the recovery window while increasing write pressure. A checkpoint is not a backup.',[
 ['Coalesce dirty work','Several updates to one page can be reflected by one later page flush. This toy pauses at each checkpoint, flushes all dirty pages, and advances its redo boundary. A longer interval can coalesce more updates but retain a longer replay suffix.'],
 ['Production checkpoints overlap work','Real engines checkpoint while activity continues and track more complex recovery boundaries. Flush pacing and full-page images affect I/O. Logs may still be retained for replication or archiving after local recovery no longer needs them. Cleanup must honor every retention consumer.']
 ],['checkpoints'],'Why can frequent checkpoints increase writes for a hot page?',['They can flush successive versions that a later flush could coalesce','They always duplicate every SQL statement','They disable the buffer pool'],0,'Short intervals repeatedly make the evolving page durable instead of combining more updates into one flush.'),
 c('redo','Replaying twice must not apply twice.','Start with a partially updated data page. Replay the durable log, then replay it again.','Recovery needs a way to recognize work already reflected in a page. Log sequence numbers help make redo safe to repeat.',[
 ['Compare page and record positions','Each toy record contains a full after-image value and an increasing LSN. Apply a record only if it is durable and newer than the page’s LSN. Advance the page LSN with the value. Repeating the same replay then skips already applied records.'],
 ['Scope of this model','All shown changes are committed. Real recovery also resolves transaction status and incomplete work; some engines use undo, while MVCC engines can leave aborted versions invisible. PostgreSQL WAL uses resource-specific redo, not this generic after-image-only algorithm. Crash recovery also needs protection against torn or corrupt pages.']
 ],['wal','pages'],'After replay, running the identical redo sequence again should…',['Repeat each business operation','Leave the recovered page unchanged','Subtract all prior changes'],1,'The page’s recovery position prevents applying the same logged change twice.'),
 c('pitr','Restore to before the logical mistake.','Replay a backup forward until just before a bad committed update. Remove one log segment to test the chain.','Recovery needs a valid base backup and the complete required log history. Replicating a mistake is not the same as keeping recoverable history.',[
 ['Choose a recovery point','The toy starts with 100 units and replays four committed changes. The third change is a destructive but valid update. Crash recovery would preserve it; point-in-time recovery can deliberately stop before it. A missing earlier segment prevents reaching later targets.'],
 ['Prove the restore path','Test backups, archived logs, keys, permissions, and restore procedures together. A copied live data directory is not automatically a consistent backup. Measure recovery time and acceptable data loss, validate application invariants, and decide how to reconcile legitimate changes after the chosen recovery point.']
 ],['backup'],'The latest replica contains an accidental deletion. Which asset can recover the state before it?',['Only another replica with the same history applied','A valid base backup plus uninterrupted logs to the desired point','A new index on the deleted table'],1,'Historical recovery data lets you stop before the bad change; a current replica ordinarily reproduces it.')
 ]},
 {slug:'maintenance',title:'Keep the engine healthy',tag:'06 / Operations',summary:'Version cleanup, compaction debt, partition pruning, and a practical query investigation.',chapters:[
 c('vacuum','An old reader can keep dead-looking data alive.','Move the oldest active snapshot across a version timeline. Vacuum only what no reader can still need.','Version retention connects transaction lifetime to space and I/O. Cleanup must preserve every supported reader’s view.',[
 ['Read the visibility intervals','Each version is visible between its creation and replacement in this simplified timeline. A version replaced before or at the oldest supported snapshot is reclaimable here. End the old snapshot and more intervals become eligible. Actual transaction-ID rules are more involved.'],
 ['Vacuum is not ANALYZE','PostgreSQL vacuum reuses dead-tuple space and performs visibility and transaction-ID maintenance; ANALYZE refreshes planner statistics. Plain vacuum usually makes space reusable inside the relation rather than returning it all to the OS. Long transactions and retention consumers can delay cleanup; monitor them as well as table growth.']
 ],['vacuum'],'Vacuum runs but cannot remove a version an active snapshot still needs. What helps?',['Investigating and ending the unnecessary old transaction safely','Running ANALYZE until the version disappears','Ignoring snapshot visibility'],0,'Cleanup cannot discard data still required by a supported reader.'),
 c('compaction','Dropping a tombstone can resurrect a value.','Merge a new run with older data. Try discarding a deletion while an unmerged older run remains.','A tombstone can disappear only when older covered data and retained readers no longer need it. Compaction correctness comes before space savings.',[
 ['Merge versions by recency','The toy chooses the highest sequence number for each key. A tombstone masks an older value. If you remove it while an older value remains outside the merge, the lookup can find that value again. Merging the complete history removes this particular risk.'],
 ['Budget the background work','Leveled and tiered strategies trade read, write, and space amplification differently. Compaction consumes I/O and temporary space; if ingestion outruns it, runs accumulate and writes can stall. Snapshots, key ranges, and lower-level overlap impose additional conditions on dropping old entries.']
 ],['compaction','lsm'],'May a compaction always discard the newest tombstone for a key?',['Yes, it contains no value','No, unmerged older values or retained snapshots may still require it','Only if the key is short'],1,'Removing the marker can expose an old value or violate a retained snapshot’s view.'),
 c('partitions','A partition only helps if you can skip it.','Select a date interval across twelve monthly partitions. Hide the usable predicate and watch every partition return.','Partitioning organizes data and lifecycle work. Pruning is valuable when predicates constrain the partition key; it does not replace useful indexes.',[
 ['Read the bounds','The toy uses one partition per month with known bounds. A recognizable date interval selects only intersecting months. Without usable bounds, every partition remains a candidate. Real planners can prune at planning or execution time under supported conditions.'],
 ['Beyond faster reads','Dropping or detaching an old partition can simplify retention. Too many partitions add planning and management overhead. Uniqueness and foreign-key rules depend on the engine and partition key. Partitioning inside one database is not automatically sharding across machines.']
 ],['partitions'],'A query does not constrain the partition key in a usable way. Must partitioning make its scan cheaper?',['Yes, more partitions always mean less work','No, the query may need every partition','Yes, each partition has zero overhead'],1,'Skipping work requires a valid exclusion argument from the query and partition bounds.'),
 c('investigation','Find the first wrong assumption.','Read a miniature execution report, choose a next measurement, and test the diagnosis.','Trace the query from estimates to actual work, waits, and maintenance. Validate one change against the representative workload.',[
 ['A useful investigation order','Check correctness and returned rows; compare estimates with actuals; identify repeated loops, reads, spills, and lock waits; inspect relevant statistics and access paths. A query returning few rows can still scan or sort many. Workload shape and concurrency belong in the report.'],
 ['Changes have a lifecycle','An index adds storage and write work. A memory increase can multiply across sessions. Schema changes can acquire locks; use engine-appropriate online operations and compatible migration stages. Bound connection concurrency, test cancellation and timeouts, and compare before/after cost with a rollback path.']
 ],['stats','memory','locks'],'A sort dominates latency and reports large temporary I/O. What is the most direct next investigation?',['Its input cardinality, ordering requirement, and memory budget','Only whether a primary key exists','The application’s source-code line count'],0,'Those observations can explain the spill and reveal whether less input, an ordered access path, or a safe memory change removes it.')
 ]}
];
